description:
## Why
user_id partitioning in mailbox_messages and user_mailboxes exists only to isolate per-user data. With a single shared mailbox there is no partitioning to enforce.

## What

### SQLite migrations
0011_drop_user_id_mailboxes.sql:
  CREATE TABLE mailboxes_new (mailbox_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, role TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE, name TEXT NOT NULL, sort_order INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 10);
  INSERT INTO mailboxes_new SELECT mailbox_id, role, name, sort_order FROM user_mailboxes WHERE user_id = (SELECT MIN(user_id) FROM user_mailboxes) OR user_id IS NULL;
  DROP TABLE user_mailboxes;
  ALTER TABLE mailboxes_new RENAME TO mailboxes;

0012_drop_user_id_messages.sql:
  CREATE TABLE messages_new (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, mailbox_id TEXT NOT NULL, envelope_from TEXT NOT NULL, envelope_to TEXT NOT NULL, raw_message BLOB NOT NULL, received_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ','now')), FOREIGN KEY (mailbox_id) REFERENCES mailboxes(mailbox_id));
  CREATE INDEX idx_messages_mailbox ON messages_new (mailbox_id);
  INSERT INTO messages_new (id, mailbox_id, envelope_from, envelope_to, raw_message, received_at) SELECT id, mailbox_id, envelope_from, envelope_to, raw_message, received_at FROM mailbox_messages;
  DROP TABLE mailbox_messages;
  ALTER TABLE messages_new RENAME TO messages;

### Postgres migrations
Equivalent 0011/0012 for migrations_pg/.

### Rust changes
- Update all sqlx queries referencing user_mailboxes → mailboxes and mailbox_messages → messages.
- Remove user_id bind parameters from all queries in provision.rs, server.rs, email/get.rs, session.rs.
- received_at DEFAULT changed to strftime RFC3339 form (fixes the P3 datetime conversion issue from review 4d9y.17).

## Acceptance criteria
- Migrations run cleanly on a fresh database and on a database with existing rows.
- No user_id column in mailboxes or messages tables.
- All existing tests pass.
